{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfMsYCaevBAqFWWAYBRR5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```json\n[\n  {\n    \"unitKey\": \"20240818-law-q13a\",\n    \"questionNumber\": 13,\n    \"part\": \"a\",\n    \"status\": \"PASS\",\n    \"textIssues\": [],\n    \"choiceIssues\": [],\n    \"answerIssues\": [],\n    \"explanationIssues\": [],\n    \"figureIssues\": [],\n    \"sourceIssues\": [],\n    \"verifiedEvidence\": [\n      \"The stem and conditions match the official problem image. This covers the 100 V air conditioner, the 5 A one-line ground-fault current, the 1/3 of the maximum permitted value, the 150 V threshold and the 0.8 s automatic disconnection. The wording of question (a) also matches. The footer shows T1-H 019, which is consistent with reviewedFromPage [19].\",\n      \"All five choices match the original: (1) 10, (2) 20, (3) 30, (4) 40, (5) 50.\",\n      \"The official answer table lists 法規 問13(a) = 4. The candidate officialAnswer is also 4.\",\n      \"Page 32 of the METI Interpretation PDF shows Article 17, paragraph 2, table 17-1. It gives three limits: 150/Ig as the base case; 300/Ig for more than 1 s up to 2 s; and 600/Ig for 1 s or less. The two relaxed limits apply to transformers that couple a high-voltage or ≤35,000 V extra-high-voltage circuit to a low-voltage circuit. This confirms the explanation's statements about the Interpretation.\",\n      \"Recalculation: 0.8 s is 1 s or less, so the limit is 600/Ig = 600/5 = 120 Ω. One third of that is 40 Ω, so choice (4) is confirmed.\",\n      \"Wrong-choice explanations recalculated: (1) 150/5 = 30 Ω, then ÷3 = 10 Ω. (2) 300/5 = 60 Ω, then ÷3 = 20 Ω. (3) 150/5 = 30 Ω. (5) 50 Ω cannot be obtained from 10, 20, 40, 30, 60 or 120 Ω. Each reason is correct.\",\n      \"The original has no figure, and none is needed to answer. figureUrls is empty, which is appropriate.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"unitKey\": \"20240818-law-q13b\",\n    \"questionNumber\": 13,\n    \"part\": \"b\",\n    \"status\": \"PASS\",\n    \"textIssues\": [],\n    \"choiceIssues\": [],\n    \"answerIssues\": [],\n    \"explanationIssues\": [],\n    \"figureIssues\": [],\n    \"sourceIssues\": [],\n    \"verifiedEvidence\": [\n      \"Question (b) matches the official problem image. This covers the ground fault, the 10 mA limit on current through a body touching the metal case, the upper limit on the Class D ground resistance, and the 6000 Ω body resistance.\",\n      \"All five choices match the original: (1) 10, (2) 15, (3) 20, (4) 30, (5) 60.\",\n      \"The official answer table lists 法規 問13(b) = 5. The candidate officialAnswer is also 5.\",\n      \"Equivalent circuit: the 100 V source feeds Rb = 40 Ω in series with Rd in parallel with Rh = 6000 Ω. The current-division result Ih = 100Rd/[Rb(Rd+Rh)+RdRh] = 100Rd/(6040Rd+240000) is algebraically correct.\",\n      \"Setting Ih = 0.01 A gives 39.6Rd = 2400, so Rd ≈ 60.6 Ω. Checking Rd = 60 Ω gives 6000/602400 ≈ 9.96 mA, which confirms it.\",\n      \"Approximate method: the case voltage is 100Rd/(40+Rd) ≤ 6000 × 0.01 = 60 V, so Rd ≤ 60 Ω. This agrees with choice (5).\",\n      \"Body currents for each choice recalculated: Rd = 10 Ω gives 1000/300400 ≈ 3.33 mA. Rd = 15 Ω gives 1500/330600 ≈ 4.54 mA. Rd = 20 Ω gives 2000/360800 ≈ 5.54 mA. Rd = 30 Ω gives 3000/421200 ≈ 7.12 mA. All match the stated values.\",\n      \"The only Interpretation-based input is Rb = 40 Ω from (a), which is confirmed on page 32 (Article 17, table 17-1). The explanation makes no other independent claims about the Interpretation.\",\n      \"The original has no figure. The explanation describes the equivalent circuit well enough to follow without one, and figureUrls is empty, which is appropriate.\"\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":42586,"cache_read_input_tokens":139443,"cache_cr